course: dictionary
lesson: 8625
title: A name that becomes a word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words start as names. Scrooge is the name of a character, Ebenezer Scrooge, in Charles Dickens's story «A Christmas Carol». The story is about a mean old man, so the name tells you what kind of man he is.
explain: Capital letters are a clue. Scrooge with a capital S is one person in a book. When people say «don't be a scrooge» with a small s, the word means something else: a mean person who hates spending money.
explain: Check by swapping in the meaning. «He refused to buy anyone a present. What a scrooge!» Put in "a mean person who hates spending money" and it still makes sense. Put in "a famous painter" and it does not.
fact: Scrooge is a proper noun when it names a person, so it starts with a capital letter.
fact: «A Christmas Carol» was written by Charles Dickens and published in 1843.
fact: In the story, Scrooge says «Bah! Humbug!» about Christmas, and he changes his ways after visits from three ghosts.
fact: As a common noun, a scrooge is an informal word for a mean person who hates spending money or does not enjoy Christmas.
fact: Dickens's Scrooge blames «a crumb of cheese» for a ghost, which is why cheese often gets blamed for nightmares, though there is no good evidence for this.
exercise: Who is Scrooge in Dickens's story? | A ghost who scares children | A town on the coast | *The mean old man who learns to be kind | A cook who makes Christmas puddings
exercise: Ella says, «Don't be a scrooge and skip buying a present for your sister.» What does scrooge mean here? | *A mean person who hates spending money | A famous writer | A kind friend who shares everything | A large, heavy meal
exercise: Which word in «Scrooge says Bah! Humbug!» is a name? | *Scrooge | Humbug | says | Bah
test: What does scrooge mean when it is a common noun? | *A mean person who hates spending money | A kind giver of presents | A ghost who visits at night | A type of Christmas tree
test: Is there good evidence that cheese gives you nightmares? | Yes, cheese always gives nightmares | Only if you eat it in winter | *No, there is no good evidence for it | Yes, Dickens proved it in his story
exam: What does Scrooge learn in «A Christmas Carol»? | To write a famous book | *To be kind and generous after visits from three ghosts | To make cheese for everyone | To hate Christmas even more
